course: world
lesson: 108
title: How long since? Counting back
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: "How long since?" asks how much time has passed from something that already happened until now. The trick is the same as "how long until": start at the earlier time and count up to now, jumping to the o'clock first.
explain: Ali says, "I've been waiting ages!" He got to the park at 3:50 and it is 4:05: 10 minutes to 4:00, then 5 more, only 15 minutes. Waiting, missing someone or wanting a cuppa makes time feel longer than the clock says.
explain: Across midday, count whole hours or switch to the 24-hour clock: the dog was fed at 7:30 am and it is 1:30 pm, which is 13:30, and 13:30 − 7:30 = 6 hours. No wonder he is whining at his bowl.
explain: Across midnight, split the time in two. Holly fell asleep at 9:45 pm and woke at 7:15 am: 2 hours 15 minutes to midnight plus 7 hours 15 minutes after it makes 9 hours 30 minutes of sleep.
explain: For days in the same month, just take one date from the other: a bean planted on 2 March that sprouts on 16 March took 16 − 2 = 14 days, two weeks, which the British call a fortnight.
fact: To find how long since something happened, count up from the earlier time to now.
fact: From 3:50 to 4:05 is 15 minutes: 10 minutes to 4:00 and 5 more.
fact: From 7:30 am to 1:30 pm is 6 hours; on the 24-hour clock that is 13:30 − 7:30.
fact: From 10:15 to 11:00 is 45 minutes, three quarters of an hour and less than one hour.
fact: A phone at 100% at 7:00 and 50% at 12:00 lost half its charge in 5 hours, about 10% every hour.
fact: From 9:20 to 10:05 is 45 minutes: 40 minutes to 10:00 and 5 more.
fact: From 20 August to 20 October is 2 months, not a year.
fact: "A minute ago" usually just means recently; from 8:03 to 8:10 is really 7 minutes.
fact: From 11:40 am to 3:10 pm is 3 hours 30 minutes: count the whole hours to 2:40, then add 30 minutes.
fact: For two dates in the same month, take the smaller date from the bigger: 16 − 2 = 14 days.
fact: A fortnight is 2 weeks, 14 days; the word is common in Britain, Ireland and Australia.
fact: When time runs across midnight, count to midnight first and then add the time after it: 9:45 pm to 7:15 am is 9 hours 30 minutes.
fact: Words people use: "ages" and "donkey's years" mean a long time, "cuppa" is a cup of tea, "gasping" means desperate for a drink, "kip" means sleep, "our kid" means my brother or sister (Manchester) and "mardy" means grumpy (north and Midlands of England).
exercise: Mia's dog was fed at 7:30 am. It is now 1:30 pm. How long since he ate? | 6 hours | 5 hours | 7 hours | 4 hours
exercise: What is 1:30 pm on the 24-hour clock? | 13:30 | 01:30 | 11:30 | 12:30
exercise: Ali got to the park at 3:50. It is 4:05. How long has he waited? | 15 minutes | 55 minutes | 25 minutes | 5 minutes
exercise: Gran's last cup of tea was at 10:15. It is 11:00. How long ago was it? | 45 minutes | 1 hour 15 minutes | 15 minutes | 2 hours
exercise: Leo's phone was 100% at 7:00 and 50% at 12:00. How long has it been going down? | 5 hours | 7 hours | 12 hours | 50 minutes
exercise: The phone lost 50% in 5 hours. About how much did it lose each hour? | 10% | 5% | 50% | 25%
exercise: The test started at 9:20 and the teacher says "Pens down" at 10:05. How long was the test? | 45 minutes | 1 hour 15 minutes | 35 minutes | 55 minutes
exercise: Sophie last saw her best friend on 20 August. It is 20 October. How long has it been? | 2 months | 1 year | 3 months | 2 weeks
exercise: Dad put the kettle on at 8:03. It is 8:10. How long ago was it? | 7 minutes | 1 minute | 13 minutes | 3 minutes
exercise: The toilets were last cleaned at 11:40. It is 3:10 pm. How long ago? | 3 hours 30 minutes | 4 hours 30 minutes | 2 hours 30 minutes | 3 hours 10 minutes
exercise: Jamie planted a bean on Monday 2 March. It sprouted on Monday 16 March. How long did it take? | 14 days | 16 days | 18 days | 7 days
exercise: How many weeks are 14 days? | 2 | 4 | 1 | 14
exercise: What is a fortnight? | 2 weeks | 4 nights | 2 months | 40 nights
exercise: Holly fell asleep at 9:45 pm and woke at 7:15 am. How long did she sleep? | 9 hours 30 minutes | 2 hours 30 minutes | 10 hours 30 minutes | 8 hours 30 minutes
exercise: How long is it from 9:45 pm to midnight? | 2 hours 15 minutes | 3 hours 15 minutes | 2 hours 45 minutes | 1 hour 15 minutes
exercise: What is the easiest way to count how long since 3:50 if it is now 4:05? | Count up to 4:00, then add 5 | Take 50 away from 5 | Add 3:50 and 4:05 together | Count back 50 minutes from 4:05
test: Ali, proper mardy: "I've been waiting ages!" He came at 3:50 and it's 4:05. What's the truth? | Only 15 minutes | Over an hour | 55 minutes | 5 minutes
test: Gran's gasping: "Not had a cuppa in hours!" Her last one was at 10:15 and it's 11:00. Is she right? | No, it's been 45 minutes | Yes, it's been 2 hours | Yes, it's been 11 hours | No, it's been 15 minutes
test: Sophie texts, "It's been, like, a year, babe," since she saw her friend on 20 August. Today is 20 October. What's the real gap? | 2 months | 1 year | 10 months | 20 days
test: Dad: "I put the kettle on a minute ago." The oven clock went from 8:03 to 8:10. What does "a minute ago" mean here? | Recently; it was really 7 minutes | Exactly one minute ago | Ten minutes ago exactly | Eight hours ago
test: Asha says the 11:40 cleaning was "donkey's years ago". It's 3:10 pm. How long has it really been? | 3 hours 30 minutes | Many years | 30 minutes | 4 hours 10 minutes
test: Our kid says, "Holly had a proper kip, 9:45 pm to 7:15 am, that's like 2 and a half hours." Put it right. | It's 9 hours 30 minutes, because it goes across midnight | It's 2 hours 30 minutes, he's right | It's 7 hours exactly | It's 12 hours exactly
test: Mia's dog, fed at 7:30 am, whines at 1:30 pm. Mia says, "He only ate an hour ago." What should she know? | He ate 6 hours ago | He ate 1 hour ago, as she says | He ate 8 hours ago | He ate 30 minutes ago
test: What would happen to Leo's phone if it kept losing about 10% an hour after 50% at 12:00? | It would be flat at about 5:00 | It would be flat at about 1:00 | It would be flat at about 10:00 at night | It would never go flat
test: Jamie's bean was planted on 2 March and sprouted on 16 March. His mate says, "A fortnight, easy." Is that right? | Yes, 14 days is a fortnight | No, a fortnight is 4 weeks | No, it was 16 days | No, a fortnight is 10 days
test: Teacher: "Started at 9:20, pens down at 10:05, so you had an hour, stop whining." Was it an hour? | No, 45 minutes | Yes, exactly an hour | No, 1 hour 25 minutes | No, 35 minutes
test: A cartoon detective says, "The cake was eaten at 11 pm and found at 1 am, that's 10 hours." Put him right. | It's 2 hours, counting across midnight | It's 10 hours, he's right | It's 12 hours exactly | It's 14 hours exactly
exam: It is given that an animal was fed at 7:30 am and that the present time is 1:30 pm. Determine the interval elapsed. | Six hours | Five hours | Seven hours | Four hours
exam: Whereas a person retired at 9:45 pm and awoke at 7:15 am, ascertain the aggregate duration of sleep. | Nine hours and thirty minutes | Two hours and thirty minutes | Ten hours and thirty minutes | Eight hours and thirty minutes
exam: Identify the number of days elapsed between the second and the sixteenth day of the same month. | Fourteen | Sixteen | Eighteen | Twelve
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the term "fortnight". | It denotes a period of two weeks | It denotes a period of four nights | It denotes a period of two months | It denotes a period of forty nights
